    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter June 20th with a lineup optimized for high-leverage counting stats, spearheaded by **Trea Turner** (69.05) and **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (67.32). **Trea Turner** is our premier play; despite facing a capable arm in Peralta, he benefits from a slow-delivery matchup that bolsters his stolen base upside, providing a massive boost to his score. Similarly, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** is a high-ceiling start against Corbin; his +15% SP Skill bonus outweighs the minor platoon penalty, making him an ideal candidate to capitalize on his elite sprint speed today. **Kyle Schwarber** (63.04) and **Yordan Alvarez** (62.94) round out our top-tier threats, with **Yordan Alvarez** providing the most reliable floor due to his elite 0.450 xwOBA, which helps mitigate the negative platoon impact of facing the southpaw Cantillo.\n\nOur most difficult bench decision involved **Luke Raley** (37.26), who fell just shy of the active roster. While **Luke Raley** offers respectable power, the optimizer penalized him heavily for a tough lefty-on-lefty matchup against Early, combined with poor park factors. We ultimately leaned toward the higher positional efficiency of **Junior Caminero** (58.99), whose superior order placement and favorable platoon split provided a significantly higher floor. We are keeping a close watch on the lineups for **Caleb Durbin**, **Trea Turner**, **Carson Benge**, and **Kyle Schwarber**; all remain \"Lineup Pending,\" and should their actual batting positions slide down the order, we risk significant efficiency loss. We remain thin on bench depth for immediate pivots, with **Christian Yelich** unavailable and both **Hyeseong Kim** and **Rece Hinds** currently assigned to the minors. With **Gary Sanchez** battling a poor BvP history against Sale, we are banking on the high-upside speed of our outfield core to carry the day.",
